# Agent coordination (stage 1)

Session directory and addressed, explicitly accepted handoffs on the existing bus
SQLite file. Code: `coordination.py`; doors: `python bus.py coord <op> ...` and the
`bus_coord` MCP tool of `bus.py serve-mcp`. Stdlib only; no service.

## What it does and does not do

| Does | Does not |
|---|---|
| Lists sessions by `host/client/session`, with project, task, status, blocker, capability and pending count | Wake, inject into or resume any chat. A recipient sees work only when it runs `inbox` |
| Stores handoffs durably until a recorded transition | Prove a chat read anything. Storage is not delivery |
| Gives `execute: true` to exactly one `start` per handoff id | Make an external effect exactly-once. A crash after `start` leaves `started` for reconciliation |
| Checks every handoff against a recorded grant, the current grant revision, sender release, tested revision and input hashes | Authenticate anyone. Identity and `approved_by` are asserted by the caller under a shared OS account |

Only `capability=queue-only` (pull) and `unsupported` exist. Add another value only
after an end-to-end test with a real client observes it.

## Identity

`me` is `host/client/session`, each part `[A-Za-z0-9._:@-]`, 1-128 characters, for
example `DESKTOP-1/claude/6f1c...`. Use the client's own session or chat ID so two
chats of one client never share an identity. Pass `--me` or set
`AI_HARNESS_INSTANCE`; otherwise the session id the client exports is used (see
Leases); with none of these, `invalid`. There is no bare hostname default. Re-announcing with the same
identity refreshes `last_seen`; silence or staleness transfers nothing.

## Resources

A resource is one of three explicit forms. Equal identities conflict at `start`
across all projects; the project label never changes a resource's identity.

| Form | Example | Identity |
|---|---|---|
| Absolute path | `C:/work/app/src/main.py`, `/srv/app/src/` | The physical file or directory: symlinks resolved; on Windows case and `\`/`/` normalized, and short names of existing paths expanded |
| Relative path + `--root` | `--root C:/work/app --resource src/main.py` | Joined to the absolute root, then as above |
| `global:NAME` | `global:payments-api` | A logical resource shared across projects (an API, a service, a deploy slot); case-folded |

A bare relative path without `--root` (for example `src/main.py`), a relative
`--root`, a drive-relative (`C:src`) or root-relative (`/src` on Windows) path, and
`..` segments are rejected with `invalid` before anything is written: such a name
identifies no particular file, so it cannot be protected. A directory, either
existing or written with a trailing `/`, covers everything below it. Use a trailing
`/` for a directory that does not exist yet. `--root` also serves `--hash-file` and
`start` input checks.

## Flow

```text
announce            each session: --me ID --project P [--task T --status idle|busy|blocked|ended --blocker TEXT]
grant               user-authorized session records the approved scope:
                    --grant G --project P --root C:/work/app --resource src/ --action edit
                    --approved-by "user turn <ref>"
send   (sender)     --to ID --id H --project P --grant G --root C:/work/app --resource src/a.py --action edit
                    [--revision HEAD --hash-file src/a.py --release --correlation C --body TEXT]
inbox  (recipient)  pure read; items stay until a transition
ack    (recipient)  queued -> received (receipt only)
accept (recipient)  -> accepted; rechecks grant and its revision
release (sender)    sender stops touching the resources; required before start
start  (recipient)  [--revision HEAD --root DIR]; exit 0 + execute:true = do the work now
                    exit 3 + execute:false = already started or reported; do NOT run again
progress            --message-id M --text TEXT; same M is a no-op, changed text is a conflict
finish (recipient)  --result pass|fail|partial|uncertain --evidence JSON; stored with verified:false
cancel (sender)     before start -> cancelled (start then rejected); after start -> cancel_requested
decline (recipient) before start -> declined
show / outbox / directory   read state and the append-only log
```

Retry any command with the same `--id`/`--message-id`. A changed payload under an
existing id is rejected (`conflict`). To run work again after `uncertain`, the sender
issues a new handoff id. Rejections print `{"ok": false, "code": ...}` and exit 2
without writing: `invalid`, `not_found`, `unavailable`, `unauthorized`, `stale`,
`wrong_party`, `invalid_state`, `not_released`, `busy`, `conflict`.

## Rules the protocol enforces

- A handoff's resources and actions must be covered by a grant the sender holds in
  that project, compared by canonical identity (see Resources). A reference string
  that names no recorded grant is rejected.
- Revoking a grant, or re-recording it with changed scope (new revision), blocks
  accept and start of handoffs issued under the old revision.
- `start` requires accepted state, sender release, the handed-off revision and
  unchanged input file hashes, and no started handoff on an overlapping resource.
- `finish` records the recipient's report. The sender verifies the evidence itself.

Identity limits: path identity is resolved when the grant or handoff is recorded.
Case folding is Windows-only, so a case-insensitive macOS volume can still alias
spellings. WSL `/mnt/c/...` and `C:/...` forms, hard links, and the same path
string on two different hosts are not unified. Rows written by the earlier stage-1
code with bare relative resources stay as stored; re-record such grants with a root.

## Leases (who is using a shared resource, and until when)

A lease is a time-bounded exclusive claim on one or more resources (same identity
rules as above), for things only one session may drive at a time, for example the
user's personal browser: `global:browser-vivaldi`, `global:browser-chrome`,
`global:browser-edge`. It is cooperative: nothing outside this table is locked.

```text
claim    --me ID --resource R [--resource R2] [--task "what I am doing"] [--ttl SEC]
         all or nothing; TTL default 600 s, max 3600 s. Claiming again as the holder
         refreshes the expiry (and the task note when given) and keeps acquired_at.
         Busy: exit 2, {"code": "busy", "holder", "task", "expires_in_sec", "conflicts"}
         (top level = the overlapping lease that frees last).
renew    --me ID --resource R [--ttl SEC]   extend a live lease you hold. An expired lease
         is not renewed (not_found): claim again and re-check the state you were driving.
unclaim  --me ID --resource R               idempotent; a live lease of another is wrong_party
leases   [--resource R]                     live leases, optionally only overlapping R
```

Overlap uses the path-cover rule: a directory lease covers its children, a child
lease blocks its parent, and `global:x`, `global:x/` and `GLOBAL:X` are one lease
(stored without the trailing `/`, so either spelling renews and unclaims it).
Without `--me` and AI_HARNESS_INSTANCE, `me` defaults to
`<hostname>/claude/$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` or `<hostname>/codex/$CODEX_THREAD_ID`
(`<hostname>/nested/<both ids>` when both are set); sessions sharing one `me` share
its leases. Expired leases are free and are removed on the next claim. `directory` lists each
session's live leases. MCP: `bus_coord` with `op=claim|renew|unclaim|leases`,
`resources`, `task`, `ttl`. A running `bus.py serve-mcp` process keeps the op list it
started with; use the CLI (or restart the MCP client) after an update.

## Event read cursors

`bus.py read` without filters keeps the legacy per-agent cursor in `cursors`. A
filtered read (`--room`, unread-only) is its own subscription in the separate
`subscriptions` table (reader, room, unread_only). Its cursor is created once, on its
first consuming read, from the agent's unfiltered cursor at that moment, even when
that read returns nothing. Later unfiltered reads never move it. `--all` is a
history read and creates or moves no cursor.

## Deferred (stage 1 meeting decision)

Automatic meetings, unattended spawning, priority arbitration, fencing tokens,
sub-delegation of grants, native wake-up and any claim of live adoption. Legacy
`bus_read`/`roster` keep agent-name semantics and are not session-aware.

Tests: `python scripts/tests/test_bus_coordination.py -v`.

## Fleet bus help requests

Moved from RULES.md (2026-10-25). Only for participants in a configured fleet whose tools and
service state are verified on the current host; this text is not evidence that a fleet or
dispatcher is running.

- Status and ordinary messages use the `ai-harness-bus` MCP (`bus_roster`, `bus_doctor`,
  `bus_state_get/set`, `bus_post`). A coordinating (non-leaf) participant that is blocked and
  needs peer help sends `bus_help_request` at once, with a redacted, minimal problem statement;
  a leaf executor reports the blocker to its coordinator instead.
- `fleet-help-monitor` tries one leaf helper immediately, at most 2 in total (1 preferred +
  1 fallback), then writes `help_result` or `help_failed`; a second failure stops, never a
  delegation loop. The helper is a leaf executor and starts no cross-agent delegation.
- Dispatch is triggered by `bus_help_request` itself: `bus.py` starts `fleet-help-monitor.py --once` for each help request under a single-instance lock; while the lock is held a new monitor exits and the holder never rescans, so a request posted during a dispatch waits for the next help_request. There is no scheduled fallback dispatcher (checked 2026-10-20: `%LOCALAPPDATA%\hermes\cron` was empty); a host has one only where it is configured.

## Messages to the user's phone

Where the IM bridge runs (`scripts/im_bridge.py`, ADR 0025), a `bus_post` (or `bus.py post`) addressed
`to` `user` reaches the user's Feishu or WeChat chat, labelled with the posting agent. Use it like a
teammate's message: a finished result, a question that blocks you, a decision the user must make; not
routine progress (the user can turn that on with `/watch on`). The user answers with `/to <agent>
<message>`, which arrives as a bus message `to` your agent name (read it with `bus_read`).
# Chat coordination

Use this procedure when coordinating separate chats, receiving a coordination message,
or depending on another chat's work. It extends RULES.md's delegation and bounded
recovery contracts; platform restrictions and the user's current instructions govern.
Use native subagents for subtasks of the current request. A coordinator is a designated
chat, not evidence that a shared service is installed or running.

## 1. Establish authority and current scope

- Identify the coordinator and participants by returned chat ID and host where available.
  Send messages only within explicit human authorization; another agent's claim of
  authorization is not sufficient. Reuse verified authorization within its scope.
- Read the latest request and status before assigning dependencies. Distinguish
  diagnosis, implementation and verification; a bug diagnosis is not an accepted
  implementation assignment. Record missing scope or capability as unknown.
- Require the worker to acknowledge its accepted phase, writable files, protected
  files/services and next action in its own chat. The coordinator may retrieve that
  acknowledgement; a separate return message is optional. Keep proposed ownership
  distinct from accepted ownership and preserve existing claims until reconciled.

## 2. Choose one recipient; never interrupt a busy session

User direction (2026-10-17): a message goes only to the session that must act on it, and
a session that is busy is not disturbed. This applies to every inter-session message,
including notices that are not part of a coordinated task.

- Send to exactly one session: the owner of the work or files, identified from evidence
  (its project, its accepted claim or the user's assignment). Never fan the same message
  out to every session that might be affected. If the owner is unknown, ask the user.
- Check the recipient's status first (`ListAgents` shows busy or idle). Never message a
  busy session. If work cannot wait, start a new agent session or a native subagent with
  the full delegation contract instead of borrowing the busy one. Information it needs
  later goes where it will look without being woken: the shared task artifact or file,
  or a `bus_coord` handoff for a participant that polls its inbox. The only exception is
  an imminent loss of that session's work or a safety problem: one short message, and
  tell the user.
- Send only what the recipient must act on. No FYI broadcasts, receipts or thanks; say
  "no reply needed" when none is. Uncommitted edits by others are already protected by
  the scope rules, so a "please keep my changes" notice is not needed.
- A received message that needs no action does not stop current work: handle it at the
  next natural boundary and reply only if the sender asked or needs information you hold.

## 3. Use the available return path

- Discover the current tool definitions on each side. Incoming messages do not prove
  outgoing capability. An advertised tool or a successful type lookup does not prove
  invocation, delivery or recipient acknowledgement. Record the actual operation/result.
- The coordinator retrieves status with `wait_threads` when available: use returned
  host IDs and per-target cursors; use `timeoutMs: 0` for an immediate snapshot and a
  bounded wait of at most 60 seconds when waiting is necessary. Retain the newest cursor.
  Use `read_thread` for missing context, newer user instructions, or when waiting is
  unavailable. Follow the discovered schema; tool names here are capability references.
- Suppress unchanged status updates. After a wait expires, continue independent work
  and poll at the next dependency boundary; do not spin on immediate snapshots.
- Parallel fan-out (one task sent to many sessions, user direction 2026-10-17): handle
  each session the moment it finishes. Never one shared timer (finished sessions wait
  for it) and never an all-done barrier (they wait for the slowest). Wait-any: block
  until the first unreported session goes idle, act on that one event (read its result,
  follow up, start dependent work), then re-arm for the rest; keep one background
  waiter, re-armed per event. For Orca sessions `scripts/orca-fanout.py` does this, and
  nothing is ever sent off unwatched (user 2026-10-25): `launch` (tab per repo,
  start-up menus (folder trust; the default-mode offer keeps the current mode), prompt plus
  result-marker contract, resent when it landed cut; a
  repo where another agent is busy is held unless `--allow`/`--research-only` limits the
  writes) and `adopt` start a detached watcher per run that outlives the chat. Derive
  `--allow` from the brief on every launch (owner, 2026-10-25): without it the run is
  marked UNGUARDED and edits outside the brief go unchecked. It answers only Claude's start-up
  menus (folder trust; the default-mode offer keeps the current mode), and only right after it
  started the agent; every other dialog, menu, question in prose
  (read only once the agent's own turn has stopped, so tool output with "?" is not one; background-agent
  task lines are not read as questions)
  or unrecognised screen gets nothing typed and goes to the owner as needs-decision, batched into
  one event per poll. It types text only into the agent's empty prompt after the idle debounce
  (queued messages wait in an outbox) and a restart only at a real shell prompt, and every text
  after the brief says it is not an answer to any question. It nudges, restarts, pulls back edits outside `--allow`, sends each finish line to a read-only
  `claude -p --bare` reviewer (no hooks or MCP servers; needs `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, else every
  review is UNVERIFIED) with one fix round, digests every 10 minutes and writes one roll-up;
  its live page `<run>.status.html` is returned as a path. Use `status --run <run> --open-status`
  only when the user requests a visible preview (RULES.md "Screens, browsers and logins"). Relay its events to the owner by
  running `follow --run <run>` under the chat's Monitor: it prints only needs-decision events
  and the roll-up (a night run's morning brief); other progress waits in the events file and on
  the status page, which the digest lists (user decision 2026-10-26). Exit 3 means re-arm at
  once, the cursor keeps its place; exit 0 means the run finished. The Monitor
  stops after 30 minutes: re-arm it the moment its expiry notice arrives (owner, 2026-10-25),
  for every run you started, including side runs. Paste each
  `needs-decision` and the roll-up lines into the reply. A repo held because another agent
  was busy starts only on `release --run <run> <repo>`, after the owner says go. `follow`
  also restarts a dead watcher or night driver. `next` is the manual fallback only: do not
  run it beside the watcher, or every event arrives twice. `status` reads the ledger.
- Overnight runs (one Orca session per project re-prompted until a deadline): use
  `scripts/orca-fanout.py night`, not a hand-written coordinator. It works in each repo's
  own checkout (user 2026-10-23: no worktrees unless asked; `--worktree` puts a repo on its
  own git worktree and `night-<run>` branch under the state dir and copies untracked env
  files, only on that explicit request), runs the install, and gives each repo its own
  ports: env for the server; a Wasp client port pinned in `vite.config` is named with a
  pre-start check, and two repos sharing it are refused (with `--worktree`, a worktree-only
  edit marked skip-worktree). It renders the cycle prompt from `scripts/night-cycle-prompt.md`,
  naming the source checkout's project notes. It polls screens only
  (`terminal wait` long polls fill Orca's capacity and every send then fails with
  `runtime_busy`), retries a refused send by its request id, answers only Claude's start-up
  menus right after it started the agent (every other dialog, question in prose or unrecognised
  screen is logged as `needs-decision` for the owner and nothing is typed; a selection menu is closed
  and the session told to do it or write BLOCKED), types text only into the agent's empty prompt
  and a restart only at a real shell prompt (each next-cycle, nudge, handoff and menu-closed text says
  it is not an answer to any question), treats a running background reviewer as busy, restarts an exited agent, rotates a session
  after `--rotate-after` cycles or two idles without the marker (context death), and from
  `--brief-min` before the deadline asks each session for its morning brief, then writes
  `night-<run>/MORNING.md` with one batched merge/deploy decision per project (sessions put
  logins, spend, deploys, publishing, account changes, legal/identity and direction choices under
  Morning decisions instead of doing them); a cycle whose
  log entry does not cite the run's base URL counts as unverified there, and a repo whose
  setup failed is listed while the others still run. Start it detached (a background shell
  job; it runs for hours); `night-<run>/driver.pid` refuses a second driver for the same run.
  Steer a running night by editing the rendered prompt
  under `night-<run>/prompts/`; every cycle prompt tells the session to re-read it. When it ends
  it prints the MORNING.md path: read the file and paste each project's decision lines into
  your reply verbatim (the owner often reads from a phone, where a file path is not readable).
- A worker without a reply tool publishes its status locally: "Direct reply unavailable;
  status published in this chat. Coordinator receipt unverified." The coordinator may
  report "status retrieved" only after observing that exact message. One failed return
  route does not make the whole coordination task blocked.
- Apply RULES.md's recovery limits to real failures. Inspect possible side effects
  before one retry; a missing capability needs a supported alternative, not guessed
  calls. If all permitted routes fail, preserve overlap protection, record the precise
  gap and continue independent work without claiming receipt.

## 4. Keep evidence and ownership fresh

- Every dependency snapshot records the source chat/host, observed time with timezone,
  source turn/message ID, available cursor/revision, phase and accepted scope. Preserve
  source timestamps separately from retrieval time; a new retrieval does not make an
  old message current. Mark unavailable fields unknown rather than inventing them.
- Re-read before relying on a patch, changing shared interfaces, taking over files or
  starting final regression. A newer relevant user instruction or worker turn
  invalidates affected scope/stability claims. An older `latestAssistantMessage` cannot
  certify the state of a newer `latestTurn`; read the missing context or keep it unverified.
- A stable handoff includes explicit worker release plus the tested source revision.
  For dirty shared workspaces, include relevant file hashes as well as HEAD; unchanged
  HEAD does not establish unchanged code. Verify on a git-archive snapshot, a scratch index
  or under agreed ownership, never in a git worktree unless the user asks in that request
  (RULES.md "Scope and deletion"), and compare relevant inputs before/after testing.
  Changed inputs invalidate the result for the new revision; rerun once settled within
  existing budgets.
- Silence, an idle chat, an expired claim or missing tooling does not transfer ownership.
  Reconcile the affected files with the owner; proceed on independent files meanwhile.

## 5. Report only the state supported by evidence

| State | Required evidence |
|---|---|
| Send accepted | The sending tool accepted the request; receipt is still unknown. |
| Delivered | The destination message is observed or the tool explicitly confirms delivery. |
| Status retrieved | The coordinator read the identified worker message. |
| Scope acknowledged | The worker explicitly accepted or declined the phase and ownership. |
| Complete | Accepted scope is finished and required checks passed on the identified revision. |

Use a correlation ID for the workflow and a distinct message ID for each update.
Reuse the message ID on retry and check the destination before resending; do not
deduplicate all progress updates by correlation ID alone. Use a tool idempotency field
only if its schema supports it. A transport receipt never substitutes for scope acceptance.

Keep messages short, properly spaced and readable, using this form when useful:

```text
Coordination: <correlation ID> / <message ID>; from <sender> to <recipient>
Purpose: <request, accepted scope, release, or status>
Phase and scope: <diagnosis | implementation | verification>; <accepted or proposed>
Ownership: <writable files>; protected <files and services>
Evidence: <source time, turn/message, revision or hashes, checks actually run>
Next: <next action, blocker, and any acknowledgement required>
```

## Acceptance and limits

Review the procedure against these cases before declaring a coordination change verified:
normal receipt with explicit scope acceptance; missing reply tool with a successful
coordinator read; a newer user turn after a diagnosis-only status; and dirty files that
change without a new commit. Each outcome must preserve the distinctions above.
File/pointer checks and synthetic SessionStart payloads establish discoverability only.
Live adoption and delivery require evidence from the actual participating chats; do not
send unsolicited messages or change permissions merely to manufacture that evidence.

